    "evidence_text": "IL-23\u2013responsive innate lymphoid cells are increased in inflammatory bowel disease\n\nThe relevance of ILC populations in human health and disease is currently poorly understood.\n\nThe induction of such cells in response to gut flora may be a mechanism protecting normal individuals against inflammatory bowel disease.\n\nCCR7 provides localized access to IL-2 and defines homeostatically distinct regulatory T cell subsets\n\nIn situ regulation of DC subsets and T cells mediates tumor regression in mice.\n\nMice lacking the transcription factor T-bet in the innate immune system develop microbiota-dependent colitis.\n\nInnate lymphoid cells: emerging insights in development, lineage relationships, and function.\n\nILCs function in lymphoid organogenesis, tissue remodeling, antimicrobial immunity, and inflammation, particularly at barrier surfaces.\n\nWe also discuss the potential roles of ILCs in the pathology of immune-mediated inflammatory and infectious diseases including allergy.\n\nIntestinal bacteria and the regulation of immune cell homeostasis.\n\nIn contrast, CD14(-) DCs promoted differentiation from CD127(+) ILC1 toward ILC3.\n\nDendritic cells (DCs) are crucial for mounting allergic airway inflammation, but it is unclear which subset of DCs performs this task.",
